        ["The lateral, posterior, and medial cords of the brachial plexus are named for their position relative to which structure?",["Axillary artery","Clavicle","Coracoid process","Subclavian vein"],"The cords are named by their relation to the axillary artery behind pectoralis minor. This landmark helps place the plexus in a mental map of the axilla."],
        ["A long-distance cyclist has numbness of the small finger and weak finger abduction, but normal sensation on the dorsal ulnar hand. Where is the ulnar nerve most likely compressed?",["Guyon canal at the wrist","Cubital tunnel at the elbow","Between the heads of pronator teres","Interscalene triangle"],"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch leaves the nerve above the wrist, so dorsal sensation is spared in Guyon canal compression. A cubital tunnel lesion would also numb the dorsal ulnar hand."],
        ["After a fall on an outstretched hand, a patient has tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox but a normal initial X-ray. What is the most appropriate step?",["Immobilize and refer for follow-up imaging for possible scaphoid fracture","Begin wrist mobilization to restore extension","Reassure the patient that a normal X-ray rules out fracture","Start grip strengthening and return to activity"],"Scaphoid fractures are often missed on first X-rays and carry a risk of avascular necrosis. Snuffbox tenderness calls for immobilization and repeat imaging."],
        ["Which structures pass through the quadrangular (quadrilateral) space?",["Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery","Radial nerve and the deep (profunda) brachial artery","Suprascapular nerve with the suprascapular artery","Circumflex scapular artery and thoracodorsal nerve"],"The quadrangular space transmits the axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery. The radial nerve and profunda brachii pass through the triangular interval, and the circumflex scapular artery uses the triangular space."],
        ["An overhead athlete has wasting of infraspinatus only, with normal supraspinatus bulk and abduction strength. Where is the suprascapular nerve MOST likely compressed?",["Spinoglenoid notch","Suprascapular notch","Quadrangular space","Interscalene triangle"],"The branch to supraspinatus leaves before the spinoglenoid notch, so compression there spares supraspinatus. A lesion at the suprascapular notch would weaken both muscles."],
        ["Across full shoulder elevation, glenohumeral and scapulothoracic motion are classically described in about which overall ratio?",["2:1","1:1","4:1","1:2"],"The classic overall scapulohumeral rhythm is about 2 degrees of glenohumeral motion for each degree of scapular upward rotation. The ratio varies by person and phase, so it is taught as an approximation."],
        ["After a fall, a patient has weak elbow flexion and forearm supination with numbness along the lateral forearm. Which nerve is MOST likely injured?",["Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Axillary nerve"],"The musculocutaneous nerve supplies biceps and brachialis and ends as the lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve. Radial injury would weaken extensors, not elbow flexion and supination together."],
        ["Which muscle works with serratus anterior as the main force couple for upward rotation of the scapula?",["Trapezius","Rhomboid major","Levator scapulae","Pectoralis minor"],"Upper and lower trapezius pair with serratus anterior to upwardly rotate the scapula during elevation. Rhomboids, levator scapulae, and pectoralis minor are downward rotators."]
