      gen: [],
      qs: [
        ["Which structures pass through the carpal tunnel?",["Median nerve and the FDS, FDP, and FPL tendons","Median nerve, ulnar nerve, and the FDS tendons","Median nerve, radial artery, and the FCR tendon","Ulnar nerve with the FDP and palmaris tendons"],"The carpal tunnel contains the median nerve and nine tendons: four FDS, four FDP, and FPL. The ulnar nerve uses Guyon's canal, and FCR runs in its own groove."],
        ["A patient has numbness of the thumb, index, and middle fingers with a positive Phalen test, but normal sensation over the thenar eminence. What BEST explains the spared thenar skin?",["The palmar cutaneous branch passes superficial to the retinaculum","The thenar skin is supplied by the superficial radial nerve","The recurrent motor branch carries the thenar skin fibers","Thenar skin is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve"],"The palmar cutaneous branch leaves the median nerve above the wrist and runs over the flexor retinaculum, so it escapes carpal tunnel compression. A proximal median lesion would affect it."],
        ["A cyclist has weak finger abduction and numbness of the small finger, with normal sensation on the dorsal ulnar hand. Why does the dorsal sensation stay normal?",["The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch leaves above the wrist","The dorsal hand is supplied by the radial nerve only","Guyon's canal holds only the motor fibers of the nerve","The median nerve supplies the dorsal ulnar hand"],"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch arises 5-8 cm above the wrist, so compression in Guyon's canal spares it. A cubital tunnel lesion would numb the dorsal ulnar hand too."],
        ["Which muscles are supplied by the median nerve in the hand?",["Lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis, APB, and superficial FPB","All lumbricals, opponens pollicis, adductor pollicis, and APB","Palmar interossei, opponens pollicis, APB, and FPB","Lateral two lumbricals, adductor pollicis, and hypothenar muscles"],"LOAF: lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis. Adductor pollicis and the interossei are ulnar."],
        ["Which muscles abduct the fingers away from the midline of the hand?",["Dorsal interossei","Palmar interossei","Lumbricals","Extensor indicis"],"Dorsal interossei abduct (DAB) and palmar interossei adduct (PAD). Lumbricals flex the MCPs and extend the IPs."],
        ["After a laceration, a patient's ring and small fingers rest with the MCP joints hyperextended and IP joints flexed. Loss of which muscles BEST explains this posture?",["Interossei and medial two lumbricals","Flexor digitorum superficialis to digits 4-5","Extensor digitorum to digits 4-5","Hypothenar muscles of the small finger"],"The ulnar-supplied interossei and medial lumbricals normally flex the MCPs and extend the IPs. Without them, extrinsic extensors hyperextend the MCPs and long flexors flex the IPs, producing a claw."],
        ["A basketball player jammed a fingertip and now cannot actively extend the DIP joint, which rests in flexion. Which structure is MOST likely disrupted?",["Terminal extensor tendon","Central slip of the extensor","Volar plate of the PIP joint","Flexor digitorum profundus"],"Loss of the terminal tendon causes mallet finger, a drooping DIP. Central slip injury gives a boutonniere deformity at the PIP instead."],
        ["Two weeks after a blow to the dorsum of the PIP joint, a patient's finger shows PIP flexion with DIP hyperextension. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?",["Boutonniere deformity from central slip rupture","Swan-neck deformity from volar plate laxity","Mallet finger from terminal tendon rupture","Jersey finger from FDP avulsion"],"Central slip rupture lets the lateral bands slide below the PIP axis, flexing the PIP and pulling the DIP into hyperextension. Swan-neck is the opposite pattern."],
        ["A patient cannot form an \"OK\" sign: the thumb IP and index DIP joints stay extended, and sensation is normal everywhere. Which nerve is MOST likely injured?",["Anterior interosseous nerve","Median nerve at the carpal tunnel","Ulnar nerve at Guyon's canal","Superficial branch of the radial nerve"],"The anterior interosseous nerve is purely motor to FPL and the lateral FDP, which flex the thumb IP and index DIP. A carpal tunnel lesion would cause numbness and thenar weakness instead."],
        ["A patient has weak finger and thumb extension at the MCP joints, but can still extend the wrist, which deviates radially. Sensation is normal. Where is the lesion MOST likely?",["Posterior interosseous nerve at the supinator","Radial nerve in the spiral groove","Superficial radial nerve at the wrist","C7 root in the cervical foramen"],"ECRL is supplied before the radial nerve enters the supinator, so wrist extension survives with radial pull. A spiral groove lesion would cause full wrist drop and dorsal hand numbness."],
        ["Which carpal bone is MOST at risk for avascular necrosis after a fracture through its waist?",["Scaphoid","Lunate","Capitate","Hamate"],"The scaphoid's blood supply enters mostly distally, so a waist fracture can cut off the proximal pole. The lunate is more often dislocated than fractured."],
        ["A patient has forearm aching with numbness of the lateral three and a half digits AND over the thenar eminence; symptoms worsen with resisted pronation. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?",["Pronator syndrome","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Anterior interosseous syndrome","Guyon's canal syndrome"],"Thenar skin numbness means the palmar cutaneous branch is involved, which places the lesion above the wrist; resisted pronation points to pronator teres. Anterior interosseous lesions cause no sensory loss."],
        ["A pregnant client in her third trimester reports night-time tingling in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. What is the MOST appropriate initial intervention?",["Neutral wrist night splint with activity modification","Corticosteroid-free deep friction over the carpal tunnel","Immediate referral for carpal tunnel release surgery","Resisted wrist flexion strengthening three times daily"],"Fluid retention in pregnancy raises pressure in the carpal tunnel, and symptoms often resolve after delivery. A neutral night splint reduces nighttime pressure; surgery is reserved for persistent or progressive cases."],
        ["Which structure forms the medial boundary of the carpal tunnel and the lateral boundary of Guyon's canal?",["Hook of the hamate","Tubercle of the scaphoid","Ridge of the trapezium","Head of the capitate"],"The hook of hamate anchors the medial flexor retinaculum and forms the radial wall of Guyon's canal. A fracture of the hook, common in golfers, can irritate the ulnar nerve."],
        ["A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has fingers with PIP hyperextension and DIP flexion. What is this deformity called?",["Swan-neck deformity","Boutonniere deformity","Mallet deformity","Ulnar drift deformity"],"Swan-neck is PIP hyperextension with DIP flexion, often from volar plate laxity or intrinsic tightness in RA. Boutonniere is the reverse pattern."]
      ]
